@Helen Wright
I've said this before, but:
....I hated and feared math, but memorized my way through as much as possible and took the least number of classes...  then in college, forced to enroll in Algebra, I had a wonderful prof who actually explained the number system!...and suddenly I understood the useless things like "Invert and multiply"....  at last I knew what was really going on and why!  I loved it and took extra maths (to escape having to take a language, as I was studying for a BS.)  It made me a good math teacher, because I knew every possible way one could be confused....

@Mean Old Lady
That’s a great story, I wish I’d had access to a decent teacher. Sadly, my State school was in a run down area and did not attract the best teachers. My maths teacher (and I use that word carefully) thought girls had no place in his classroom. So I stopped going. He didn’t even notice until I was caught sneaking out of school about four months later. This was the early 70’s when you couldn’t challenge a teacher.
 It was only in my 50’s, seeking help for my youngest who also struggled, that I discovered we both have dyscalculia. He got masses of help from some great teachers. I was just happy to know I wasn’t as deficient as I’d been made to feel.

